<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/intermediate/personality-theories">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Personality psychology asks <strong>what makes people stable over time and different from
    each other</strong>. Its modern answer: a handful of measurable, continuous dimensions — led
    by the Big Five — grounded in factor analysis, twin studies and longitudinal data. It is the
    branch of psychology where measurement discipline most clearly separated science from
    pop-psychology.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="how the Big Five was built, what it measures, and what it replaced" widget="equationStep" config={personalityCfg} />

  <h2>Findings with consequences</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Genetics ~40–60%:</strong> twin studies are consistent across cultures; the remainder is mostly non-shared environment (the part siblings DON'T share) — a finding that surprised everyone (Plomin).</li>
    <li><strong>Stability with slow change:</strong> traits are stable but not frozen — conscientiousness rises through adulthood (maturity principle), neuroticism declines slightly.</li>
    <li><strong>Situations still matter:</strong> traits predict behaviour averaged over time, not any single act (Mischel's 1968 challenge, resolved as "if…then" patterns — personality as stable situation-behaviour signatures).</li>
    <li><strong>Use in hiring is contested:</strong> conscientiousness predicts job performance modestly; faking, bias and legal exposure complicate practice. Personality tests inform; they don't decide.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Goldberg (1981); McCrae & Costa (Five Factor model); Roberts et al. (2007) on trait change; Mischel (1968).</p>
</TopicPage>
